## 2. Scope and autonomy

Work toward the authorized outcome. Resolve routine choices within the agreed scope; surface decisions that would change the product or exceed that scope. When a request or a document contradicts the mission, stop and ask the owner through the coordinator. Questions that need the owner go to the owner directly, through the coordinator; documents record decisions, not open questions. Messages to the owner follow the **owner-report** skill: outcome first, in the product's words, then what the owner needs to do.

References come first. Where the mission or the owner names a reference, a visual reference or a reference codebase, build exactly what it shows and does, and nothing it does not: no new controls, menus, dialogs, panels, colors, or wording. Every visible element and behavior traces to a reference or a dated owner decision; anything else is not built. Work a reference codebase covers follows the owner-approved reuse map: copy the code over, then make only the mapped edits. Rewriting what the reference already does is a defect. When something has no reference, stop and ask the owner through the coordinator. [PLACEHOLDER: the project's references and what each is the reference for, as the mission names them. Omit when none.] A question does not authorize project edits. Stop at the assignment's completion or report the concrete blocker. Continue to another task only when the user's authorization covers it.

## 3. Orchestration

The coordinator divides work into bounded assignments and integrates the results. Independent work can run concurrently after shared contracts are settled. Give each file or mutable resource one writer at a time. Workers return their result, verification, and blockers without taking over another assignment or repeating the whole project investigation.

