## 4. Plans and records

PLAN describes milestones and dependencies; TODO is the short checklist of current work. The coordinator uses the **plan** and **todo** skills when their creation is needed within authorized work, and maintains them as progress or blockers change. Write important decisions into their owning document when settled; conversation alone is not a durable record. Only the owner changes the mission; ideas from discussion stay proposals until the owner adds them.

The coordinator uses the **audit** skill for one brief timestamped record at a milestone boundary or when coordinated work stops blocked or is abandoned. Record actual participants, significant attempts, results, and remaining work. Preserve earlier audits. Workers supply evidence rather than separate logs. No hourly updates or progress screenshots are required.

The coordinator uses the **diary** skill for a developer diary entry when the user asks for one, or accepts an offer at the end of a working session. An entry covers all work since the previous entry and the next steps intended in the discussion. It links audits rather than repeating them, and its next steps do not authorize work.

A project that starts from nothing works toward version 1.0 unless the owner states otherwise. Version 1.0 is the full realization of its pitch. A version's working documents, including its pitch, live under `.project/documents/development/<version>/`. The pitch says what the version delivers toward the mission and what it leaves for later; it does not redefine the product. When the version's work is complete, the coordinator writes its post-mortem with the **post-mortem** skill and moves the version to `.project/documents/history/<version>/`.

## 5. Verification

Keep the existing working user journey intact. Use the **tests** skill when writing, changing, or reviewing tests. During a slice, add no new tests; run the existing scripted suite once when the change is complete. Tests for approved behavior are written in one pass when the owner asks to commit. Do not repeat passing checks without a concrete reason. Report what was implemented, what was actually checked, and what remains unverified. Never invent user approval.

